  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will shift \ntoward the interplay between large scale troughing over the \neastern Pacific/West Coast and the subtropical high over the \nSouthern Plains and Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor \nretrogression toward the Desert Southwest approaching the weekend.\nThis evolution would not completely scour residual moisture \nimmediately, so rain chances will likely linger into Thursday. \nCurrent PoPs show 30-50% chances for foothill and mountain areas \nand 10-30% probabilities for the South- Central Arizona deserts. \nBeyond that, persistent southwesterly flow would eventually dry us\nout enough to limit rain chances heading into the weekend. \nAnother influx of moisture by the end of the seven day period may \nbring rain chances back once again, but max chances at this time \nonly range between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will \nonly fluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the\nridge keeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region,\nas mentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to \nour west will keep the high from becoming too amplified, \npreventing any further discussion of abnormal heat, at least \nduring this forecast period. Temperatures for the lower deserts \nThursday through Sunday will range generally between 94-106 \ndegrees, while overnight lows hover near 70-82 degrees.",
